{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"57fe9852-c418-4026-886f-d04001d183df","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still failing with the same error. Let me debug this more carefully. The regex is:\n\n```python\noutput_section_match = re.search(\n    r'##\\s+输出格式\\s*\\n(.*?)(?=^##\\s|\\Z)',\n    content,\n    re.MULTILINE | re.DOTALL,\n)\n```\n\nWith `re.MULTILINE`, `^` matches the start of any line. And `re.DOTALL` makes `.` match newlines.\n\nSo it matches `## 输出格式\\n` and then `(.*?)` captures everything until the next line that starts with `## `. But wait, the regex pattern `^##\\s` means: start of line, then `##`, then a whitespace character.\n\nInside my markdown code block (triple backticks), there are lines like:\n```\n### BusinessEntity:\n```\n\nWith `re.MULTILINE`, `^### BusinessEntity:` matches `^##\\s` because:\n- `^` - start of line\n- `##` - the first two # chars\n- `\\s` - the space after `###`\n\nYes! Lines inside the code block like `### BusinessEntity:` are at the start of lines in the raw text and match `^##\\s`. So the regex still stops at the first occurrence inside the code block.\n\nI need a different approach. The problem is that the regex can't distinguish between `##` headings and `###` headings, AND it also can't skip content inside markdown code blocks.\n\nOptions:\n1. Change the format of the template section to avoid `### ` at the beginning of lines inside the code block\n2. Use a different format for the section structure\n\nLet me go with option 1: I'll indent the code block content so that `### ` doesn't appear at the start of a line. But that would break the markdown code block formatting...\n\nActually, a simpler approach: I can use `**TypeName**：` inside the code block instead of `### TypeName:`. But the test needs to find `### TypeName:` patterns...\n\nWait, the test checks within output_section:\n```python\nfor t in builtin_types:\n    pattern = rf'###\\s+{t}[:\\s]'\n    if re.search(pattern, output_section):\n        group_titles_found.append(t)\n```\n\nIf the output_section is truncated before reaching the code block containing `### BusinessEntity:`, then none of these will be found.\n\nThe fundamental problem is that the regex `(?=^##\\s|\\Z)` stops at ANY line starting with `##`, including `### ` lines inside the code block.\n\nTwo fixes:\n1. Change the test regex (but I'm not supposed to modify the test)\n2. Change my content so there are no `### ` at line start within the section\n\nI think the right approach is to avoid using `### ` inside the code block. Instead, I can use an alternative format. Since the test is checking for `### BusinessEntity:` etc. as group titles, and the code block content with `### BusinessEntity:` is INSIDE triple backticks (it's a template, not the actual section content), I need to find a way around this.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The test checks:\n1. Section content > 80 chars\n2. All 7 type group titles `### TypeName:` are found\n\nFor #1, I need the section to not be truncated before sufficient content.\nFor #2, I need `### TypeName:` to appear somewhere in the section.\n\nThe problem is that the code block with `### BusinessEntity:` etc. is causing the regex to truncate before reaching the code block content.\n\nSolution: Don't put the template in a code block. Instead, describe it in plain text, or use `<pre>` tags, or indent it. But indenting would still have `### ` at line start if I'm not careful.\n\nAlternative solution: Use `<pre>` HTML tags instead of markdown code blocks. Inside `<pre>`, the regex wouldn't match `### ` because... wait, it would. The raw text still contains `### ` at line start.\n\nBetter solution: Use a `>` blockquote for the template section. But that still has `### ` at line start.\n\nActually, the cleanest solution: prefix the template lines with 4 spaces (indented code block in markdown). In an indented code block:\n```\n    ### BusinessEntity:\n```\nHere, `### ` is no longer at position 0 (line start), so the regex `^##\\s` won't match!\n\nWait, with `re.MULTILINE`, `^` matches at the start of each line, which is position 0 of each line. `    ### BusinessEntity:` starts with spaces, then `### `. So `^` matches position 0 (the first space), and `^\\s*##\\s` would match, but `^##\\s` would NOT match because position 0 is a space, not `#`.\n\nSo using a 4-space indented code block would fix the issue!\n\nBut wait - the pattern the test uses for group titles is:\n```python\npattern = rf'###\\s+{t}[:\\s]'\nif re.search(pattern, output_section):\n```\n\nThis searches for `### BusinessEntity:` without the `^` anchor. So even inside an indented code block, it would find `### BusinessEntity:` as long as the output_section contains that text.\n\nIf I use 4-space indented code blocks, the output_section would NOT be truncated by the `### ` lines, and the patterns would still be found. This should work!\n\nLet me convert the markdown code block (triple backticks) to a 4-space indented code block.","signature":"57fe9852-c418-4026-886f-d04001d183df"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":210,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":60032,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1976cb09-8947-476e-ba19-a6bdb5c374b8","uuid":"1f84f7c1-0532-4d60-b0e8-43760c8aa981"}
